'''''Podocarpus acutifolius''''', commonly known as '''needle-leaved tōtara''', is a species of [[conifer]] in the family [[Podocarpaceae]].<ref name="TeAra">{{cite web |title=Story: Conifers |url=http://www.teara.govt.nz/en/conifers/4 |publisher=Te Ara – the Encyclopedia of New Zealand |access-date=21 August 2012}}</ref> This species is [[endemic]] to New Zealand.

== Etymology ==
The [[Binomial nomenclature|specific epithet]], {{lang|la|acutifolius}}, meaning "thorny leaves", is derived from [[Latin]] {{lang|la|acutus}} (pointed, acute), and {{lang|la|-folius}} (-leaved), and refers to the characteristic shape of the leaves.<ref name="Eggli Newton 2004">{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=u2n5vusQ1DEC&pg=PA2|title=Etymological Dictionary of Succulent Plant Names|last1=Eggli|first1=Urs|last2=Newton|first2=Leonard E.|publisher=Springer|year=2004|isbn=978-3-540-00489-9|location=Berlin, Heidelberg|page=2|access-date=19 November 2018}}</ref>

== Distribution ==
This species is found in the South Island from northern [[Marlborough Region|Marlborough]] westward and south to southern [[Westland District|Westland]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite book|url=https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q63848025|title=Eagle's complete trees and shrubs of New Zealand volume one|last=Eagle|first=Audrey|publisher=Te Papa Press|year=2008|isbn=978-0-909010-08-9|location=Wellington|pages=10|language=English}}</ref> Its natural [[habitat]] is lowland and [[Montane ecosystems|montane]] forest and scrub.<ref name=":0" />

